            "orgs/gftdcojp/cloud-itonami/src/cloud_itonami/media/batch.clj"
            "90-docs/adr/2607110200-kawaraban-r0-r1-cloud-itonami-isco-3521-media-broadcast.md"]}

 :problem
 {:source "same-session follow-up to ADR-2607110200 addendum 1 (2026-07-10 CPU-time-limit
            read fix): continuing the owner's 'wire up periodic execution' instruction,
            validated kawaraban.run-live-ingest with a real 20-outlet backfill (584
            articles published) before wiring a GitHub Actions cron"
  :observed
  ["immediately after the 584-article backfill, yoro-social-v2 became completely
    unresponsive: direct kotobase.aozora.app datoms reads got ZERO HTTP response at 60s,
    90s, 280s, and 340s timeouts -- worse than addendum-1's incident, which at least
    returned a 503/error-1102 within ~49s or a 200 within 129s post-fix"
   "pds.aozora.app listRecords (the AVET-narrowed production read path, not the raw
    unbounded datoms scan addendum-1 also fixed) was ALSO unresponsive at 30s -- AVET
    narrowing only accelerates lookups against the COLD (already-folded) snapshot; all
    584 new records exist only in unfolded novelty, so reads touching them pay the full
    novelty-replay cost regardless of index narrowing"
   "wrangler tail on app-aozora-pds showed the */5 * * * * fold cron firing on schedule
    (15:55, 16:00, 16:05, 16:10 JST) but every invocation reported \"Unknown\" status,
    never the clean \"Ok\" seen before -- consistent failure across 4 consecutive
    attempts, not a one-off flake"]
  :root-cause
  ["kotobase-peer.core/fold! (core.cljc:1452) is, and always has been, an O(total graph
    size) full rebuild -- its own docstring already says so. Addendum 1's fix (bounded
    pmap-async batching + raised CPU budget) reduced the CONCURRENCY cost of that
    rebuild, not its TOTAL cost, which scales with the entire shared graph's size since
    inception, not with how much new novelty a given fold call is compacting"
   "fold! -> hydrate-db (core.cljc:1117) reads one full index tree (spo) via
    cold-datoms with (constantly true) -- scans and decrypts EVERY entry in the cold
    snapshot, every single fold call, regardless of new-novelty size"
   "fold! -> qs/commit! (arrangement.core:183) -> index-root -> prolly-tree.core/
    build-tree (prolly_tree/core.cljc:106) -- prolly-tree.core exposes exactly
    build-tree/lookup/scan-prefix. THERE IS NO INCREMENTAL INSERT/UPDATE PRIMITIVE.
    Every commit of the 4 indexes rebuilds each tree from its complete freshly-sorted
    entry set"
   "yoro-social-v2 is the shared cross-actor AppView index (YORO_DB_NAME/
    YORO_OPERATOR_DID) every actor's createRecord projects into (per
    app-aozora-pds/worker.cljc's own scheduled-fold docstring), not kawaraban's own
    graph -- its total size grows with the whole ecosystem's lifetime history, not with
    kawaraban's write volume alone"]}

 :decision
 {:paused "the originally-requested GitHub Actions periodic-execution wiring
           (live-ingest.yml / media-digest.yml) is NOT created, and no further bulk
           write batches against yoro-social-v2 are run, until fold is fixed --
           continued periodic writes against an already-unrecoverable backlog would
           only make the eventual fix harder without benefit, since reads are already
           broken for anything written since the last successful fold"
  :incremental-fold-not-implemented-this-session
  "the real fix is DESIGNED here but deliberately not implemented now -- owner
   instruction: scope it as its own piece of work rather than rush it under time
   pressure into a foundational shared library (kotobase-peer/arrangement/prolly-tree)
   that minidrama, tashikame, and the AppView itself all depend on"
  :other-deliverables-kept
  "kawaraban.run-live-ingest's and cloud-itonami.media.batch's high-water-mark/dedup
   logic (this session's other work) are NOT reverted -- still correct and necessary
   for bounding FUTURE write volume once fold is healthy again, just not yet wired to
   run on a schedule"
  :design
  {:phase-1-memoized-hydration
   "cache hydrate-db's expensive decrypt-and-scan result in R2, keyed by indexed-cid
    (the cold snapshot's own content-address -- a natural cache key, only changes when
    a fold actually succeeds). Every RETRY of a fold against the same still-unfolded
    indexed-cid currently re-pays the full hydrate cost from scratch; caching stops
    that redundant repeated cost (today's tail showed 4 consecutive failed attempts,
    i.e. ~4x the necessary hydrate cost paid for zero forward progress). Does not by
    itself guarantee the FIRST hydrate completes in one budget, but is low-risk, high-
    value, and a natural first increment"
   :phase-2-chunked-checkpointed-hydration
   "the real fix for 'graph too big for one invocation': make cold-datoms's underlying
    scan resumable across multiple Worker invocations -- persist a cursor (last-seen
    prefix/key position) plus partially-accumulated decrypted entries to R2 after each
    bounded batch, have fold! check for and resume an in-progress checkpoint before
    starting fresh. prolly_tree.core/scan-prefix already supports prefix-bounded range
    scans (used today by cold-datoms's indexed reads) -- the groundwork for a resumable
    cursor exists, needs to be exposed and driven across invocation boundaries instead
    of consumed synchronously within one"
   :phase-3-deeper-alternative-not-recommended-first
   "a true incremental prolly-tree insert primitive in prolly-tree.core (structural-
    sharing insert of a bounded delta into an existing tree, avoiding build-tree's
    full-rebuild entirely) -- theoretically 'correct' fix matching what prolly/Merkle-
    search trees are designed for, but a genuine data-structure algorithm project
    across a shared library with its own correctness surface. Only worth pursuing if
    phase-1+2 stop keeping pace with real growth"}}

 :consequences
 {:no-data-lost "kawaraban's mirror articles and cloud-itonami's one published media
                 digest are append-only content-addressed R2 writes -- exist regardless
                 of fold status. The problem is read latency/availability, not
                 durability"
  :production-reads-degraded "pds.aozora.app reads (including the AVET-narrowed
                               listRecords path) are currently unresponsive for any
                               record written since the last successful fold -- today,
                               effectively all 584 of this session's backfilled
                               articles plus whatever else other actors wrote in the
                               interim"
  :periodic-wiring-blocked "the originally-requested GitHub Actions cron workflows are
                            blocked on this fix landing -- tracked as a follow-up, not
                            abandoned"
  :incidental-fix-kept "kawaraban.aozora/jvm-http-fn fixed in passing (same repo, this
                        session) to have an explicit 120s request timeout via a shared
                        pooled HttpClient -- previously had none at all, unlike the
                        fetch-side client, making a genuinely stuck request
                        indistinguishable from a slow-but-progressing one. Kept
                        regardless of this ADR's broader fold issue as an
                        independently-correct robustness fix"}

 :alternatives
 [{:option "keep raising limits.cpu_ms further"
   :rejected_because "not a durable fix -- the graph's total size is unbounded and
                       monotonically increasing, any fixed ceiling is eventually
                       exceeded again, just later. Useful as a stopgap (as addendum 1
                       was), not a design"}
  {:option "shard yoro-social-v2 into smaller per-collection or per-time-window
            sub-graphs"
   :rejected_because "not rejected outright, but out of scope here -- changes
                       cross-actor query semantics (getAuthorFeed/firehose currently
                       rely on one shared index) and is a bigger structural change than
                       incremental fold; worth a separate ADR if incremental fold alone
                       proves insufficient once actually measured against real growth
                       rates"}
  {:option "implement the full incremental-fold fix right now, in this session"
   :rejected_because "owner instruction -- touches kotobase-peer/arrangement/
                       prolly-tree, shared foundational libraries with other real
                       consumers (minidrama, tashikame, the AppView itself), deserves
                       proper scoped implementation + testing, not a rushed patch on
                       top of an already-long session that already caused one
                       production incident today"}]}
